A reporting stack of three classes all answers the same question, and a caller holds only the topmost type:
class A { String who() { return "A"; } }
class B extends A { String who() { return "B"; } }
class C extends B { String who() { return "C"; } }
A ref = new C();
System.out.println(ref.who());
What is printed?

The bottom class of a three-level chain adds nothing of its own:
class A { String who() { return "A"; } }
class B extends A { String who() { return "B"; } }
class C extends B { }
A ref = new C();
System.out.println(ref.who());
What is printed?

A superclass builds a label by calling a helper, and the subclass supplies a different helper:
class P { String label() { return "[" + mark() + "]"; } String mark() { return "P"; } }
class Q extends P { String mark() { return "Q"; } }
P ref = new Q();
System.out.println(ref.label());
What is printed?

A developer tries to replace a locked-down tag in a subclass and the compiler refuses the class:
class R { final String tag() { return "R"; } }
class S extends R { @Override String tag() { return "S"; } }
public class Main { public static void main(String[] args) { System.out.println(new S().tag()); } }
What happens when this is compiled and run?

A subclass override asks its parent for a starting number and then adds its own:
class C1 { int total() { return 1; } }
class C2 extends C1 { int total() { return super.total() + 10; } }
C1 ref = new C2();
System.out.println(ref.total());
What is printed?

A dispatcher declares a variable, leaves it unset, and calls through it anyway:
class A { String who() { return "A"; } }
class B extends A { String who() { return "B"; } }
A ref = null;
try {
    System.out.println(ref.who());
} catch (RuntimeException e) {
    System.out.println(e.getClass().getSimpleName());
}
What does this program print?

A caller overrides one method on the spot and then hides the result in a base-typed variable:
class D { String greet() { return "hi"; } }
D ref = new D() {
    String greet() { return "anon"; }
};
System.out.println(ref.greet());
What is printed?

A parent class in package one declares tag() with no access modifier, and a child class in package two declares a public tag() with the same signature:
package one;
public class Base2 { String tag() { return "one"; } }
package two;
public class Child2 extends one.Base2 { public String tag() { return "two"; } }
And in package one, Main does:
Base2 ref = new two.Child2();
System.out.println(ref.tag());
What does this program print, and why?

A subclass constructor tries to hand its own field to its parent and the compiler refuses the class:
class P1 { P1(int w) { System.out.println("P1:" + w); } }
class P2 extends P1 {
    int width = 4;
    P2() { super(width); }
}
public class Main { public static void main(String[] args) { new P2(); } }
What happens when this is compiled and run?

A parent class offers only a one-argument constructor, and a subclass declares a constructor with no call:
class U1 { U1(int v) { System.out.println(v); } }
class U2 extends U1 { U2() { } }
public class Main { public static void main(String[] args) { new U2(); } }
What happens when this is compiled and run?

A subclass constructor passes a computed value to its parent:
class V1 { V1(int v) { System.out.println("V1:" + v); } }
class V2 extends V1 {
    V2() { super(2 * 3 + 1); System.out.println("V2"); }
}
public class Main { public static void main(String[] args) { new V2(); } }
What does this program print?

A class gives itself an instance field and routes one constructor through another:
class W1 { W1(int v) { System.out.println("W1:" + v); } }
class W2 extends W1 {
    int tag = 1;
    W2() { this(9); System.out.println("W2 tag=" + tag); }
    W2(int v) { super(v); System.out.println("W2(" + v + ")"); }
}
public class Main { public static void main(String[] args) { new W2(); } }
What does this program print?

A class computes a static field through a helper and also announces each object it builds:
class X1 {
    static int counter = bump();
    static int bump() { System.out.println("class loaded"); return 1; }
    X1() { System.out.println("X1 made"); }
}
public class Main { public static void main(String[] args) { new X1(); } }
What does this program print?

A subclass constructor is missing the statement that hands its two arguments to the parent:
class Z1 { Z1(int w, int h) { System.out.println(w + "x" + h); } }
class Z2 extends Z1 {
    Z2(int w, int h) {
        ______;
        System.out.println("square? " + (w == h));
    }
}
public class Main { public static void main(String[] args) { new Z2(3, 5); } }
Which statement fills the blank so that the program prints 3x5 and then square? false?

An abstract base declares the field and the shared calculation, and a subclass supplies only the rate:
abstract class Meter {
    protected int units;
    abstract int rate();
    int bill() { return units * rate(); }
}
class Flat extends Meter { int rate() { return 2; } }
Meter m = new Flat();
m.units = 5;
System.out.println(m.bill());
What does this program print?

A ticket hierarchy shares a fee calculation and leaves the per-ticket fee to each ticket type:
______ {
    abstract int fee();
}
class Standard extends Ticket { int fee() { return 10; } }
public class Main { public static void main(String[] args) { Ticket t = new Standard(); System.out.println(t.fee()); } }
Which header completes the declaration so that the program compiles and prints 10?
